<h1>What neuroscience teaches us about how space influences the brain</h1>
<p class="meta">Spaces That Matter — Blog &amp; Resources</p>
<p class="lead">A review of 40 studies published between 2018 and 2024 on the impact of noise, light and spatial density on cognitive function. The results are unequivocal — and change the way we should think about office design.</p>
<p>Environmental neuroscience — or “neuroarchitecture” — has been a rapidly growing field of research since the 2010s. It looks at how the physical characteristics of space affect the human brain: its cognitive functions, its emotional state, its behaviors.</p>
<p>What this discipline teaches us about workspaces is both fascinating and practical. Fascinating because the mechanisms are often counter-intuitive. Practical because the implications for office design are concrete and immediately actionable.</p>
<p>Here is a summary of the main lessons drawn from 40 studies published between 2018 and 2024.</p>

<h2>Noise: the silent enemy of cognition</h2>
<p>Research on the acoustic impact of workspaces is among the most thoroughly documented. The findings converge on several points:</p>
<p>Background conversation is the most disruptive noise. It is not the overall sound level that matters most, but speech intelligibility. An office at 65 dB with non-verbal background noise (street noise, ventilation) disrupts concentration less than an office at 55 dB with partially audible conversations. The brain uses cognitive resources to filter out conversations — resources that are then no longer available for the main task.</p>
<p>A study from Cornell University (Kim &amp; de Dear, 2013) showed that employees in open-plan offices report 66% more difficulty concentrating than those in enclosed offices. A 2022 meta-analysis (Nassauer et al.) covering 23 studies confirms that daily exposure to conversation noise above 55 dB reduces performance on complex cognitive tasks by 28% on average.</p>
<p>Technical acoustic solutions are not enough. Acoustic partitions, sound-absorbing ceilings and sound masking improve objective measurements — but do not fully offset the subjective impact of noise on how people feel. The perception of control over the acoustic environment (being able to move to a quiet space, having a headset, choosing when to be in the office) matters as much as objective acoustic quality.</p>

<h2>Light: much more than a matter of visual comfort</h2>
<p>The impact of light on the brain goes far beyond simply being able to see clearly. Natural light is a major circadian synchronizer — it regulates the internal biological clock, which in turn regulates dozens of physiological and cognitive functions.</p>
<p>A Northwestern University study (Boubekri et al., 2014), now a classic of neuroarchitecture, compared employees working in offices with and without windows. The results: those exposed to natural light slept on average 46 minutes more per night, reported better sleep quality, and showed higher levels of vitality and physical activity.</p>
<p>More recently, a 2023 meta-analysis covering 18 studies (Smolders &amp; de Kort) confirmed the positive impact of natural light on cognitive performance, particularly on sustained attention tasks (+14% on average) and working memory (+9%).</p>
<p>The color temperature of artificial light also plays a role. “Cool” light (5,000-6,500K) more strongly activates the melanopic system and promotes alertness — beneficial in the morning, but potentially counterproductive late in the day. “Warm” light (2,700-3,500K) promotes relaxation and creativity.</p>
<p>“Designing office lighting solely for visual performance is like designing a car solely for top speed — ignoring comfort, safety and durability.”</p>

<h2>Density and spatial crowding</h2>
<p>The concept of “crowding” (perceived overcrowding) is distinct from objective density. What matters is not the number of people per square foot, but the perceived lack of space — which is influenced by ceiling height, the presence of open views and the ability to control one's immediate space.</p>
<p>The work of Evans and Johnson (2000), regularly cited in recent literature, showed that exposure to an environment perceived as overcrowded raises cortisol levels (the stress hormone), reduces frustration tolerance, and leads to what researchers call “helplessness” — a feeling of powerlessness that reduces motivation and initiative.</p>
<p>Conversely, spaces with high ceilings activate more abstract and creative modes of thinking — a phenomenon documented by Meyers-Levy &amp; Zhu (2007) as the “cathedral effect”.</p>

<h2>Plants and biophilia: more than a decorating trend</h2>
<p>The biophilia hypothesis (Wilson, 1984) holds that human beings have an evolutionary need to connect with nature. Decade after decade, research has confirmed and refined this impact.</p>
<p>A 2023 meta-analysis covering 22 studies in workplace settings (Lee et al.) shows that the presence of plants in the workspace is associated with a 37% reduction in stress, a 15% increase in self-reported productivity, and improved indoor air quality. Plants absorb volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and raise humidity levels — with a documented impact on concentration and on reducing headaches.</p>
<p>“Views of nature” — even partial ones, even through a window — also have a measurable effect. A 2022 study (Largo-Wight et al.) shows that employees with a view of outdoor greenery have chronic stress levels 23% lower than those without a view of nature.</p>

<h2>What this means for decision-makers</h2>
<p>The lessons of neuroscience are not academic abstractions. They have concrete implications for every office design decision:</p>
<p>Prioritize access to natural light when assigning workstations (and don't reserve it for executive spaces). Invest in acoustic solutions that reduce the intelligibility of conversations rather than the overall sound level. Provide enough quiet retreat spaces (recommended ratio: 1 phone booth for every 10 workstations). Treat plants as infrastructure, not decoration. Design spaces that give people a sense of control and choice — even in denser environments.</p>
<p>These decisions are not luxuries. They are documented performance levers — measured in terms of concentration, well-being, absenteeism and, ultimately, talent retention.</p>

<h2>The limits of objective data</h2>
<p>One last important observation: all these studies measure average impacts on populations. But the experience of space is fundamentally subjective and individual. What one employee experiences as comfortable and stimulating, another may experience as stifling or distracting.</p>
<p>That is why measuring the employee experience — as Spaces That Matter does — complements objective data rather than replacing it. It captures what sensors cannot measure: lived experience, the perception of control, the sense of belonging. And it is precisely this experience that best predicts engagement and real performance.</p>
